# SYNOPSIS

```usage
make [-eiknprSstd] [-f file] [-j jobs] [macro=value ...] [target ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`make` brings each <target> up to date, or the first target of the makefile when none is given.
It reads the rules from each `-f` <file> in order, or from `makefile`, or failing that `Makefile`, in the current directory.
A target is remade when it does not exist, when a prerequisite is newer, or when a prerequisite was itself remade; its prerequisites are brought up to date first.
It is sbase's `make`, linked alone as `/boot/bin/posix/make` rather than into the box that holds sbase's other commands: the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) binds `/boot/bin/posix` before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program finds it there.

A makefile holds rules, `target ... : prerequisite ...` followed by command lines each beginning with a tab, or by `;` and a command on the same line; macro definitions, `name = value`; comments from `#`; and `include` lines, which read another file in place.
A line ending in a backslash goes on to the next.
`$(name)` and `${name}` are replaced by the macro's value, `$(name:a=b)` by its value with each word's ending <a> changed to <b>, and `$$` by `$`.
In commands, `$@` is the target, `$<` the prerequisite an inference rule found, `$*` the target without its suffix, and `$?` the prerequisites newer than the target.

Macros come, from weakest to strongest, from `make`'s defaults, the environment, the makefiles, `MAKEFLAGS` and the command line; with `-e` the environment wins over the makefiles.
Each <macro>`=`<value> given is also exported to the commands' environment.
`SHELL` and `MAKEFLAGS` are never taken from the environment, and `SHELL`, `/bin/sh` by default, is never exported.
The flags given are passed to recursive `make`s in `MAKEFLAGS`.

Inference rules make a target from a file of the same name with another suffix, for the suffixes listed by `.SUFFIXES`; the defaults (`make -p` prints them) build `.o` from `.c`, `.y`, `.l` and `.f` with `c99`, `yacc`, `lex` and `fort77`.
The special targets `.DEFAULT` (the rule for a target no other rule makes), `.IGNORE` and `.SILENT` (with no prerequisites, for every target; with them, for those), `.PRECIOUS` (targets not removed on an interrupt) and `.SUFFIXES` (with no prerequisites, clearing the list) are understood.

Each command line is run as `$SHELL -ec` <line>, after its macros are expanded, and printed first.
A line beginning with `@` is not printed, one beginning with `-` has its failure ignored (and runs with `-c` alone), and one beginning with `+` runs even with `-n`, `-q` or `-t`.
A failing command stops `make`; when a signal stops it while a target is being made, that target is removed unless it is precious or a directory.

: `-d`
  Print what `make` decides, and why.
: `-e`
  Let environment variables override macros the makefiles define.
: `-f` <file>
  Read rules from <file>; `-` is standard input.
  May be given more than once.
: `-i`
  Ignore every command's failure, as if each began with `-`.
: `-j` <jobs>
  Accepted and ignored: one command runs at a time.
: `-k`
  When a target fails, go on with the targets that do not depend on it.
: `-n`
  Print the commands without running them.
: `-p`
  Print every macro and rule, then exit without making anything.
: `-q`
  Run nothing, and exit 1 if any target is out of date, 0 otherwise.
: `-r`
  Forget the default suffixes, and so the inference rules.
: `-S`
  Stop at the first failure, undoing an earlier `-k`.
: `-s`
  Print no commands, as if each began with `@`.
: `-t`
  Touch out-of-date targets, with `touch`, instead of running their commands.

